{"name":"Recollection Blue","hex":"#1a525b","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"recollection","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The act of recollecting, or recalling to the memory; the act of recalling to memory.","The power of recalling ideas to the mind, or the period within which things can be recollected; remembrance."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Process of collecting again."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/recollection"},{"term":"blue","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a blue hue.","Depressed, melancholic, sad."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The colour of the clear sky or the deep sea; the colour midway between green and violet in the visible spectrum and one of the primary additive colours.","Anything coloured blue, especially to distinguish it from similar objects differing only in colour."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/blue"}],"entries":[{"title":"Recall (memory)","blocks":[{"text":"Recall in human memory refers to the mental process of retrieving information from the past. Along with encoding and storage, it is one of the three core processes of memory. There are three main types of recall: free recall, cued recall and serial recall. Psychologists test these forms of recall as a way to study the memory processes of humans and animals. Two main theories of the process of recall are the two-stage theory and the theory of encoding specificity."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Recall_(memory)","wikidata":"Q18733"},{"title":"Blue","blocks":[{"text":"Blue is one of the three primary colours in the RGB (additive) colour model, as well as in the RYB colour model. It lies between violet and cyan on the spectrum of visible light. The term blue generally describes colours perceived by humans observing light with a dominant wavelength between approximately 450 and 495 nanometres. The clear daytime sky and the deep sea appear blue because of an optical effect known as Rayleigh scattering. An optical effect called the Tyndall effect explains blue eyes. Distant objects appear more blue because of another optical effect called aerial perspective."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg/330px-Earth-in-true-color-himawari-09.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blue","wikidata":"Q1088"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Ebbinghaus2.jpg/500px-Ebbinghaus2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Ebbinghaus2.jpg/500px-Ebbinghaus2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hermann Ebbinghaus","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AEbbinghaus2.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cerebellum.gif/500px-Cerebellum.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fa/Cerebellum.gif/500px-Cerebellum.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Cerebellum highlighted in red","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACerebellum.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hippocampus.gif/500px-Hippocampus.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/Hippocampus.gif/500px-Hippocampus.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Hippocampus highlighted in red","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHippocampus.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Spreading-activation-graph-1.png/500px-Spreading-activation-graph-1.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign